 On the strengths side, teams repeatedly exploited BO's sample efficiency in low-data regimes, recovering strong candidates within a handful of iterations relative to design-of-experiments or random baselines~\cite{frazier_tutorial_2018, shahriari_taking_2016}. The submissions collectively demonstrated flexibility in handling continuous, categorical, mixed, molecular, multi-objective, multi-fidelity, contextual, batched, and cost-aware problems, including successful prototypes of multi-objective search and preference-based, human-in-the-loop workflows~\cite{lin_preference_2022}. Specialized kernels and representations (e.g., for molecules) further extended BO to structured inputs~\cite{griffiths_gauche_2024}.
 
-The projects also surfaced well-known limitations. Default BO was not always superior to simple baselines: two teams reported little advantage over random search on their tasks (projects 33 and 1), and while these two-day comparisons were not run at benchmark rigor, they underscore the value of always including a simple baseline~\cite{liang_benchmarking_2021}. Results were sensitive to molecular representation, featurization, and kernel choice; warm-start data quality, observation noise, and high-dimensional search spaces were recurring pain points, for which noise-robust and heteroscedastic-aware methods are relevant remedies~\cite{aldeghi_golem_2021, altamirano_robust_2023}. Acquisition-function optimization itself mattered: one of the top-ranked projects (project 3) showed that investing more computation in the acquisition optimizer changed observed performance, connecting to ongoing work on scalable and high-dimensional acquisition strategies~\cite{ament_unexpected_2023, maus_local_2023}. Finally, retrospective benchmark gains do not guarantee wet-laboratory success, and reproducing comparisons across studies remains difficult~\cite{liang_benchmarking_2021}. Where teams integrated LLMs, the models lowered access barriers and helped specify spaces or retrieve priors, but they added another uncertain component that can degrade on complex objectives and requires validation against non-LLM baselines~\cite{kristiadi_sober_2024}.
+The projects also surfaced well-known limitations. Default BO was not always superior to simple baselines: two teams reported little advantage over random search on their tasks (projects 33 and 1), and while these two-day comparisons were not run at benchmark rigor, they underscore the value of always including a simple baseline~\cite{liang_benchmarking_2021}. Results were sensitive to molecular representation, featurization, and kernel choice; warm-start data quality, observation noise, and high-dimensional search spaces were recurring pain points, for which noise-robust and heteroscedastic-aware methods are relevant remedies~\cite{aldeghi_golem_2021, altamirano_robust_2023}. Acquisition-function optimization itself mattered: one of the top-ranked projects (project 3) showed that investing more computation in the acquisition optimizer changed observed performance, connecting to ongoing work on scalable and high-dimensional acquisition strategies~\cite{ament_unexpected_2023, maus_local_2023}. Batch size and computational overhead also mattered in practice: project 15 found that the best batch size balances model retraining time against experimental overhead, and project 37 cut fitting time by modeling yield alone and computing the objectives from the predicted yield afterwards. Finally, retrospective benchmark gains do not guarantee wet-laboratory success, and reproducing comparisons across studies remains difficult~\cite{liang_benchmarking_2021}. Where teams integrated LLMs, the models lowered access barriers and helped specify spaces or retrieve priors, but they added another uncertain component that can degrade on complex objectives and requires validation against non-LLM baselines~\cite{kristiadi_sober_2024}.
 
